2026-08-25T00:00:00Z (RFD66606-1125) 'Two prose gates, one for each kind of writing
 # uuid: 0db2cd4b-6811-5075-9694-76f13f8ad370
 # tags: rfd:title, rfd:attest-in:none
 RFD:Discussion  1 RFD
 Serials:Weftspun

2026-08-25T00:00:01Z (RFD66606-1125) 'prose enforcement, and which rule reads which kind of writing
 # tags: rfd:feature, rfd:no-final-newline
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:02Z (RFD66606-1125) '`1000-conventions/README.md`, `tropes-removal-model`
 # tags: rfd:scope, rfd:no-final-newline
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:03Z (RFD66606-1125) 'Technical writing answers to ASD-STE100, and an essay answers to the AI-trope table.
 # tags: rfd:hook
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:04Z (RFD66606-1125) 'RFD66606-1000 requires ASD-STE100 for every document here, and that rule was written for one kind of writing but is applied to two.
 # tags: rfd:intrigue
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:05Z (RFD66606-1125) 'Mark each section with its gate (RFD66606-2361), and run the table against the corpus first (RFD66606-2362).
 # tags: rfd:exit
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:06Z (RFD66606-1125) 'Two prose gates, one for each kind of writing
 # tags: rfd:details-title, rfd:no-final-newline
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:07Z (RFD66606-1125) '**Technical writing answers to ASD-STE100**, through the existing enforcer: code comments, error messages, procedures, any measurement or schema.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:08Z (RFD66606-1125) '**An essay answers to `seeds/ai_trope.parquet`**, which already exists, seeded from https://tropes.fyi. A `README.md` is an essay throughout. That table assigns READMEs to ASD-STE100 today, and this RFD reverses it.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:09Z (RFD66606-1125) 'RFD66606-1000 states the rule this supersedes. RFD66606-1063 moved enforcement to a write-time hook, so this adds a hook and not a script. RFD66606-1124 gives the measure-first rule, and the reason these gates read an AST. RFD66606-2361 marks sections, and RFD66606-2362 measures the AI-trope table.
 # tags: rfd:related
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:10Z (RFD66606-1125) 'Why the rule was split
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:11Z (RFD66606-1125) '<!-- gate: tropes -->
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:12Z (RFD66606-1125) 'The blanket rule was not wrong when it was written. It was written for one kind of document, and the repository then grew a second kind without anyone noticing that the rule had stopped fitting.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:13Z (RFD66606-1125) 'ASD-STE100 exists so a maintenance technician reading in their second language can take a procedure and act on it correctly the first time. Every constraint it carries serves that: bounded sentences, one approved term per referent, no contractions, no ambiguity about what a pronoun points at. Applied to a schema table or an install step, those constraints are exactly right.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:14Z (RFD66606-1125) 'Applied to an argument they remove the structure of its reasons. A decision persuades through how its reasons sit against each other, and a controlled vocabulary flattens that into a list of assertions. The reader loses the ability to tell a load-bearing claim from an incidental one.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:15Z (RFD66606-1125) 'The failure an essay actually has is one ASD-STE100 says nothing about. An essay fails by reading as though nobody wrote it. The tropes.fyi catalog names that failure, which is why a second gate buys something instead of duplicating the first.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:16Z (RFD66606-1125) 'The conflict this RFD resolves
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:17Z (RFD66606-1125) '<!-- gate: tropes -->
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:18Z (RFD66606-1125) '`seed_ai_tropes.py` already assigns work to each table, and it puts READMEs on the ASD-STE100 side:
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:19Z (RFD66606-1125) '    trope.parquet      ASD-STE100        READMEs, procedures, reference, rules
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:20Z (RFD66606-1125) '    ai_trope.parquet   AI-writing tells  essays, design notes, logbook entries
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:21Z (RFD66606-1125) 'This RFD moves READMEs to the other column. The disagreement belongs in the open rather than under a silent overwrite.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:22Z (RFD66606-1125) 'That assignment is defensible on its own terms. A README in most repositories is reference material, and reference material belongs under a controlled language. It is wrong here because an RFD README is not that kind of README. RFD66606-1000 asks it to state a problem and a decision in forty lines, which makes it the most compressed argument in the repository rather than the most procedural document.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:23Z (RFD66606-1125) 'The measurement decides it rather than merely permitting it. Both tables were run over the same 119 READMEs, through the same extractor:
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:24Z (RFD66606-1125) '| table                    | documents flagged | hits  |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:25Z (RFD66606-1125) '| ------------------------ | ----------------- | ----- |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:26Z (RFD66606-1125) '| `ai_trope.parquet`       | 10 of 119         | 11    |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:27Z (RFD66606-1125) '| `trope.parquet` patterns | 119 of 119        | 4,397 |
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:28Z (RFD66606-1125) 'Three orders of magnitude apart, on identical input. `Three+ Nouns in a Row` alone flags every document in the corpus. `Contraction Used` flags roughly two thirds of them, and `Semicolon Used` about a quarter.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:29Z (RFD66606-1125) 'Neither number is a verdict, and the trope side is not flattered by the comparison: its 11 hits are all false positives, as RFD66606-2362 records. These are weak-label screens from `scripts/seed_labels.py` rather than the shipped classifier, which applies thresholds these patterns do not. Read them as direction and magnitude, not as counts of real defects.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:30Z (RFD66606-1125) 'The direction is the whole argument. A gate that fails 119 documents out of 119 is not holding a standard, it is producing noise, and people stop running it. `prose_nodes.py` was written to avoid the same outcome, which arrives here through the choice of table rather than through the choice of parser.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:31Z (RFD66606-1125) 'The problem
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:32Z (RFD66606-1125) 'ASD-STE100 is a controlled language for technical writing, and a schema table is better under it. An argument is not, because a controlled vocabulary flattens an argument into a specification. An essay also fails in a way ASD-STE100 never describes, since it can read as though a model wrote it. Neither kind owns a whole file either: a `DETAILS.md` carries a measurement beside the reasoning that motivates it.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:33Z (RFD66606-1125) '2351
 # tags: rebac:verbs-from
 Text:RFD1125  1 LINE
 Text:Written

